#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106812} :activity/id #uuid "fbe08d91-7f85-5aca-90e3-9537e43e9705" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049637 :topic/id #uuid "072aa5e8-f5da-53f2-8406-4aa8a1bc1e24" :topic/math-academy-id 1263} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107064 :tutorial/content "Constants do not vary with $x.$ So, any limit of a constant evaluates to just the constant itself.\n\nFor example, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} 3=3.\n$$\n\nLikewise, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\sqrt{ \\pi}= \\sqrt{\\pi} .\n$$\n\nIn general, for any constant $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\pm\\infty} C=C.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "19052219-09a5-5548-a770-353adefe214a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2581 :tutorial/title "Infinite Limits of Constant Functions"} :step/id #uuid "45bc2482-5b40-5a90-b961-e785160dde91" :step/math-academy-id 12015 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105563 :question/id #uuid "3977d627-65bc-5f36-8c25-7d47b515f383" :question/math-academy-id "e-3566"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573934}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"} :step/id #uuid "96ffef5f-bd71-5fb3-8b87-d6ba58b7e210" :step/math-academy-id 12016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106827}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107030 :tutorial/content "Multiplication of a power function by any *positive* real constant does not influence its limit at infinity. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2{x}^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause $y=2x^2$ is an upward parabola, just like $y=x^2.$\n\n![](images/47/47d8cef2d3d189b32ee1c37f9fa563f58670d0bdc876360b13753106df03a79d.png)\n\nOn the other hand, multiplication of a power function by a *negative* real constant changes the *sign* of the limit. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2{x}^{2}=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause $y=-2x^2$ is a downwards parabola that points in the opposite direction as $y=x^2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ae2ce56-a932-54d8-a6e9-9eea329c623b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1335 :tutorial/title "The Effect of Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "17d3a58d-07af-5d54-aca7-37b0e9e2f9b5" :step/math-academy-id 2627 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106813}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107038 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2.$ To do this, we can use the graph of $y=x^2,$ which takes the familiar shape of a parabola.\n\n![](images/58/58536c20f942b37e75a1ebb60fa6298af5fa94d1ba7b2b1cd770a0251796b436.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that $y$ increases without bound as $x \\to \\infty.$ On the left side of the graph, we have a similar situation: $y$ again increases without bound as $x \\to -\\infty.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2=\\infty.\n$$\n\nNotice that the functions $y=x^4$ and $y=x^6$ have a similar shape, and as such their limiting behavior is the same.\n\n![](images/5e/5e7386db15731b3486fd64484db69e7919c8e79821d916974f689d737cf23f8a.png)\n\nIn general, whenever $n$ is an *even natural number*, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=\\infty, \\qquad \\textrm{for}\\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9981abcd-17f0-549f-9dcc-067b9bc17a0f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 190 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6a8c98da-eef2-5791-8a0c-e4700a11a8d6" :step/math-academy-id 353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105849 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105564 :question/id #uuid "88c456d9-e5d3-5b33-a847-03f201751c19" :question/math-academy-id "e-36"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050259}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562499}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"} :step/id #uuid "096e64b1-f521-5ed3-8f48-cd30efc9ff5e" :step/math-academy-id 354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106818}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106818 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107039 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at the graph of $y=x^3.$\n\n![](images/53/53d67c89fce03c6b949aecbe1e55fe8bf912ebadf1c3edc6d6c3601047886b5a.png)\n\nWe notice that the situation here is different:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} x^3=\\infty \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} x^3=-\\infty.\n$$\n\nThese limits are valid for any power function of the form $x^n,$ where $n$ is an *odd natural number*.\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=-\\infty, \\qquad n=1,3,5,\\ldots.\n$$\n\nAgain, multiplication of $x^n$ by a positive constant does not affect the limit at infinity. However, multiplication by a negative constant changes the sign of the limit." :tutorial/id #uuid "d9d4d60e-df4b-5dbb-85e6-e53ab24b16f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 192 :tutorial/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"} :step/id #uuid "c2cbae50-d421-561d-b4fe-f6dcc8a8591d" :step/math-academy-id 357 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106820}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105854 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105565 :question/id #uuid "551f2aa0-8589-5725-abe6-de1b7eca0327" :question/math-academy-id "e-37"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573882}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"} :step/id #uuid "800c9745-b1c3-57bb-8946-69f8df02ae03" :step/math-academy-id 358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107040 :tutorial/content "To compute the limit at infinity of a general polynomial, we *only* need to consider the term with the largest exponent, called the **leading term**. All of the other terms can be disregarded!\n\nFor example, to compute \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty} \\left( 6x + 2x^3-7x^2+12\\right) \\, ,\n$$\n\n we identify the leading term and compute its limit. Here, the leading term is ${\\color{blue}{2x^3}},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(6x+2{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+12) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reason this works is that when $x$ becomes very large, the behavior of the polynomial is dominated by the term with the largest exponent.\n\n**Watch out!** The leading term is *not* always the first term that appears in the expression. Rather, it is the term *with the largest exponent*. This term may appear anywhere in the expression.\n\nThe reason why we call the term with the largest exponent the \"leading\" term is that when the polynomial is arranged in standard form (with exponents ordered from greatest to least), the term with the largest exponent appears first:\n\n$$\n6x+ {\\color{blue}{2x^3}}-7x^2+12 \\quad \\to \\quad {\\color{blue}{2x^3}}-7x^2+6x+12\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "549fd7b8-2367-5c38-b26e-475d7c827a1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 196 :tutorial/title "Limits at Infinity of General Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "5e48e500-3385-5631-9674-a6c6b177bfcd" :step/math-academy-id 363 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105857 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105576 :question/id #uuid "969d5f07-c5ce-5601-b2de-0d23eb15809b" :question/math-academy-id "e-39"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522796}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "923659ef-18ec-5fc8-8fb8-ff70ca00d5c0" :step/math-academy-id 364}] :activity/title "Limits at Infinity of Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105950}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573953} :activity/id #uuid "376696e9-3932-588b-9b36-331b669d003b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049637 :topic/id #uuid "072aa5e8-f5da-53f2-8406-4aa8a1bc1e24" :topic/math-academy-id 1263} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79598fea-6ce9-502a-99b3-653e7b709cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573580}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573580} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1e5e139-150a-564f-8373-da7cbc3fc219" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d9945bb2-9891-5ac6-bca2-13e5bf3bd964" :question/math-academy-id "q-302063" :question/problem "If $p(x)=3{x}^{6}$ and $q(x)=4x,$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{{x}^{5}\\cdot q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{5}\\cdot q(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{5}\\cdot q(x)={x}^{5}\\cdot (4x)=4{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions $p(x)$ and ${x}^{5}\\cdot q(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{{x}^{5}\\cdot q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{6}}{4{x}^{6}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "10456ab3-2e7d-5daf-a3fa-916adbe6aba0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573959}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20db05d5-2cd1-5bb5-a51c-7ab499331004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573460} {:answer/id #uuid "43b84476-20bb-5bf1-ae69-7df330df7758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573507} {:answer/id #uuid "9167ac6f-2404-5e43-98e0-937156b1b81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573611} {:answer/id #uuid "94cff7bf-1bad-5286-a7d3-89bb7100fe1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573617} {:answer/id #uuid "c7eaf0c5-8dba-59d1-9c0f-0d3682cb0ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573611} :answer-field/id #uuid "38288899-0ba2-57a8-830a-6a41d6af74e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2e570ac4-c7f5-5887-a19e-4808c72f9d67" :question/math-academy-id "q-99288" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=2x$ and $q(x)=7{x}^{4},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x))?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2x\\cdot 7{x}^{4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }((2\\cdot 7){x}^{1+4}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(14\\cdot {x}^{5}) \\\\  & =14\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{5}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $14$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5} &={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(14{x}^{5})=\\infty .\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "19c5f6b1-4322-53c7-846f-39358daa684b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573952}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573959 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a2d4be-99ba-5100-b948-fcda87db3e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573429} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fb27858-d0c0-52ea-8a05-60fc113b0b6a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a3cac7e-5a2a-596f-b2a3-d5e28243a2e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-336962" :question/problem "If $r(x)=-2{x}^{8}$ and $s(x)=\\frac{11}{{x}^{10}}$, then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)\\cdot s(x))=$  \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot r(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (-2{x}^{8})=-2{x}^{10}\n$$\n\nWhen we multiply the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)$ and $s(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot r(x)\\cdot s(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-2{x}^{10}\\cdot \\frac{11}{{x}^{10}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-22 \\\\  & =-22\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "45420eee-e838-5803-81c4-d37cc13c9906"}] :activity/title "Limits at Infinity of Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573414}]}
